<p>Lyman Cary was born April 10, 1856, and grew up in Flanders, New Jersey. He graduated from Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute in New York with a civil-engineering degree.</p>

<p>Cary arrived in Mandan in April 1881 at age 25, when the railroad extended only 40 miles west of town. Like many others, he believed Mandan would become the “Omaha of the North.” Eastern investors persuaded him to send for his surveying equipment. He is credited with platting Sterling and parks in Mandan and Bismarck, often accepting development lots as payment.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/lyman-northrop-cary/379-the-arlington.jpg" alt="The Arlington, an early Mandan lodging house">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Housing was scarce. Cary and another young man found a room in a log boardinghouse with a canvas roof, reportedly the first wooden structure outside the rail yards. The roof admitted rain and snow as readily as it accommodated tenants. The two jokingly called it the Arlington after Washington's fashionable Arlington Hotel.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/lyman-northrop-cary/329-l-n-cary-handbill-front.jpg" alt="Promotional handbill for L. N. Cary's land business">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Cary initially partnered with Warren Carpenter and became a Northern Pacific Railroad land agent. Their territory extended from the Missouri River to Montana. Federal land grants had given the railroad alternating sections within 50 miles of each side of its track to offset construction risks. Agents such as Cary resold the land to settlers to finance the railway.</p>

<p>Northern Pacific recruiters encouraged European immigration. Cary sold thousands of acres for $2.60–$3 per acre, an investment buyers could reportedly recover with one good crop.</p>

<p>Cary later became land agent for the Boston Syndicate, which owned much of southwest Mandan near today's Mary Stark Elementary School, including a brickyard. He also developed major portions of downtown and reportedly completed more land-sale transactions than anyone else in North Dakota history. In the early 1900s, he offered a $100 prize for the best corn sample displayed at the Morton County Fair.</p>

<p>In 1884, Cary married Anne Alison Clark, daughter of pharmacist {% person "james-reed-clark" %}James Reed Clark{% endperson %}. They had five children: Ethelind Little, William, Anne, Alison, and Colin.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/lyman-northrop-cary/321-cary-house-1904.jpg" alt="Lyman Cary residence around 1906">
    <figcaption>Cary residence, circa 1906</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The Carys were active members of First Presbyterian Church. Their house, originally built at 201 Seventh Avenue Northwest, was moved in 1894 to 311 Third Avenue Northwest, across from the church, at Anne's request. Their son Colin and his wife later owned the adjacent house. The Cary residence was demolished in May 1989.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/lyman-northrop-cary/150-cary-lyman-pioneer.jpg" alt="Portrait of Lyman Northrop Cary">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Anne Cary is credited with suggesting that North Dakota preserve the Chateau de Morès at Medora. Her interest came from memories of the Marquis de Morès's 1885 murder indictment in Mandan, where her father served as grand-jury foreman. Anne was also credited as North Dakota's first elected woman foreperson of a trial jury. She participated in civic organizations including the Daughters of the American Revolution and Pioneer Daughters and became an authority on local history.</p>

<p>Lyman Cary died April 15, 1929. He is buried in a Union Cemetery family plot he selected while platting that part of Mandan. His son Colin operated Cary Real Estate until the mid-1960s.</p>
